Claude and ChatGPT both sell individual plans in similar steps: a free tier, a standard paid tier around $20 a month, and heavier tiers at $100 and up. The differences are in the details of how usage is counted and what sits at each rung. This guide compares the plans using only what Anthropic and OpenAI publish on their own pricing and help pages. It is not a test of which assistant is better, because we have not run either side by side. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which is the maker of one of the two products compared. The claims were then checked against the vendor pages listed below by a separate AI reviewer pass (also a Claude model), not by using the products ourselves. That reviewer could not load some OpenAI help-centre pages, and the text says where that matters. We stick to published plan facts and link every source. Plans change often, so check the live pages before you pay.

## The plans side by side

| Tier | Claude | ChatGPT |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0, limited usage | $0, limited usage on most tools |
| Entry paid | None listed | Go, $8 per month in the US |
| Standard | Pro, $20 monthly or $17 per month billed annually ($200 up front) | Plus, $20 per month |
| Heavy use | Max, $100 per month | Pro, from $100 per month |
| Top tier (paired by price, not usage) | Ultra, $200 per month | Pro tiers at $200 and $500* |

Claude's prices are from its pricing page, and its Pro help article lists $20 monthly. OpenAI's pricing documentation lists Free at $0, Go at $8, Plus at $20 and Pro from $100, with Pro plans at $100, $200 or $500 per month. The "US" qualifier on Go's price comes from OpenAI's Go announcement. *See the note on the $200 tier below.

## What the free plans include

Claude's pricing page lists limited usage every five hours and every week on Free, with chat on web, desktop and mobile, web search, file creation, code execution, memory across conversations and Artifacts. The comparison table shows Sonnet and Haiku models on Free, and Opus only from Pro up.

OpenAI's pricing page lists unlimited text chats on Free, subject to abuse guardrails, alongside limited uploads, image creation, voice chats, deep research and memory. OpenAI's "GPT-6 and other models in ChatGPT" help article says Free and Go users have unlimited everyday text chats while file uploads, image generation, voice, data analysis and other tools have separate limits.

The practical difference is in how each vendor frames limits. Claude describes a five-hour limit plus a weekly limit, with usage that depends on the length and complexity of your conversations and the model you choose. ChatGPT describes unlimited text chats but separate caps on specific tools.

## The $20 tier

At the standard tier the price is the same. Claude says Pro gives more usage than Free every five hours and every week, and adds Claude Code, unlimited Projects (Free allows up to five), more models, and Claude in Chrome and Microsoft 365. Its help article says Pro has a five-hour limit and a weekly limit, and that Pro subscribers can turn on usage credits to keep working past those limits.

OpenAI says Plus adds advanced reasoning models, expanded messages and uploads, expanded deep research, projects, scheduled tasks and custom GPTs, and early access to new features. Its usage-limits page says users who hit their limit can buy additional credits.

Two differences show up in the published lists. Claude offers a discount for annual billing on Pro ($17 per month against $20), while OpenAI's pricing page lists Plus as a monthly plan. And ChatGPT has an $8 Go plan below Plus that Claude has no equivalent for. OpenAI's Go page says that plan may include ads. In January 2026, OpenAI's Go announcement said Plus, Pro, Business and Enterprise would remain ad-free; check OpenAI's current ads page for the latest.

## The heavy-use tiers

Claude's Max costs $100 per month and, per Claude's help page, gives five times Pro's usage every five hours and every week. Anthropic's pricing page lists priority access at busy times and $100 a month in API credits. Max is monthly only, according to its help article. Ultra costs $200 and gives ten times Pro's weekly usage, with no five-hour limit for verified accounts.

OpenAI's Pro page says plans start at $100 per month, with $200 and $500 options. According to OpenAI's "About ChatGPT Pro tiers" help article, as shown in search results, the $100 tier gives five times Plus usage and the $200 tier gives twenty times. The same article carried a note that new sign-ups and upgrades to the $200 plan were temporarily paused from September 10, 2026. OpenAI's current pricing pages do not mention the pause and still list $200, and we could not load the help article to confirm whether the pause still applies, so check before planning around that tier.

## How to choose

- **Trying one out:** both vendors offer a free plan, so start there and note which limits you hit (our suggestion, not a vendor recommendation).
- **Daily use at around $20:** the price is identical, so decide on which assistant's features and limits fit your work, and test both free tiers first.
- **Light use on a tight budget:** ChatGPT Go at $8 is the only paid option under $17 in this comparison.
- **Hitting limits every day:** both vendors sell $100-plus plans, and both vendors let paid subscribers buy extra usage credits instead of upgrading: Claude's pricing table lists usage credits on Pro, Max and Ultra, and OpenAI's pricing documentation says Plus and Pro users who hit a limit can buy credits. Compare what a month of credits would cost against the next tier.

Our own suggestion, not a vendor recommendation: consider paying monthly first, since plans and limits change often. Note that Claude's Max and Ultra plans are monthly only, according to Anthropic's help pages.
